// parity-expected-stdout-begin
// [1, 1, 2, 3]
// ["a", "a", "b"]
// parity-expected-stdout-end

// GAP — owner: KI-098, Low, fix scoped to 0.0.8 (B2).
//
// NOTE: this program compiles, runs, and prints the right answer. There is no
// error to show. It is the one gap in this corpus that *no* test, parity sweep
// or snapshot here can see — the loss is in the generated code, not the
// behaviour.
//
// The only difference from ki_098_baseline_one_instantiation.flx is the second
// call, at `List<String>`. That is enough. B1 restores the representation only
// where every call site agrees on one concrete instantiation; used at two
// types, `copy_head` stays generalized and its element binder loses `IntRep`:
//
//     baseline (one call):   ::(h#1302:Int, t#1303:Box) →
//     this file (two calls): ::(h#1302,     t#1303:Box) →
//
// Downstream, Aether cannot prove the in-place reuse it could prove before, so
// the program allocates where it used to overwrite. That is why KI-098 records
// `Dups: 0 → 1`, `DropSpecs: 0 → 1`, `FreshAllocs: 1 → 2` at the seven sites it
// affects in `lib/Flow/`.
//
// **Not a correctness bug.** The generated code is slower and allocates more;
// it is not wrong.
//
// The fix is cloning, which B1 deliberately does not do: specialise at *each*
// concrete instantiation and rewrite each call site to its clone. That is owed
// to 0.0.8's B2 regardless, since generalizing constrained definitions makes
// this the common case rather than a seven-site curiosity.
//
// Observe it with:
//
//     cargo run -- --dump-core=debug examples/generics/failing/degraded/ki_098_despecialised_two_instantiations.flx
//
// Pinned by `snapshot_ki_098_despecialised_core_def` in
// tests/aether/cli_snapshots.rs. When B2 lands, this snapshot should converge
// on the baseline's — that diff is the measurement.

fn copy_head(xs) {
    match xs {
        [h | t] -> [h | [h | t]],
        _ -> [],
    }
}

fn main() with IO {
    print(copy_head([1, 2, 3]))
    print(copy_head(["a", "b"]))
}
